    <exercise number="41" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>There are many people who are single-issue voters, which means that they will vote for (or against!) a candidate based on one issue and one issue only. Suppose a politician wants to earn votes based on single issues: Issue 1, Issue 2, and Issue 3. By publicly supporting issue 1, the politician gains 127 voter but loses 154. By publicly supporting issue 2, the politician gains 350 voters but loses 83. By publicly denouncing Issue 3, the politician gains 306 voters but loses 158. By publicly taking those stances, what is the politician’s net gain or loss in number of voters?</paragraph>
      </problem>
